#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 1080.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065076} :activity/id #uuid "0dc1fb7b-6336-57cd-b507-8bd6ddfc25fc" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048189} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065076 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065433 :tutorial/content "Consider the integral\n\n$$\n\\int \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{1-4x^2}} \\, \\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis integral, apart from the coefficient of $4$ in the denominator, resembles the basic integral\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-u^2}}\\textrm{d}u = \\arcsin(u)+C.\n$$\n\nIn fact, they look almost the same, but instead of $u^2$ as the variable, we have $4x^2 = (2x)^2.$\n\nSo, let's substitute $u=2x.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm{d}x}=2\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\dfrac 1 2 \\textrm d u = \\textrm d x.\n$$\n\nWe can now write the integral in terms of $u,$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-4{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-(2x)^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(u)+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(2x)+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can often use this trick whenever we see an integral that closely resembles an integral for an inverse trigonometric function.\n\n**Note:** As always, after solving an integral using substitution, we should always double-check that our result is correct. If we differentiate the result, then it should come out to the original integrand:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(2x)+C] & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(2x)]+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-(2x)^{2}}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2x)+0 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-4{x}^{2}}}\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-4{x}^{2}}}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "53af5e98-4580-5243-a97c-de9e0d5f8fab" :tutorial/math-academy-id 863 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "d3e15f90-13b8-5437-b44e-fcdec07ad48c" :step/math-academy-id 641164 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065077}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065077 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064218 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064090 :question/id #uuid "48ec6930-88b7-5aad-9bb7-c5e23e766f80" :question/math-academy-id "e-470" :question/problem "Calculate the integral $$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\dfrac{9}{4\\sqrt{1-9x^2}} \\, \\textrm{d}x.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "This integral resembles the basic integral for inverse sine,\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-u^2}}\\textrm{d}u = \\arcsin(u)+C.\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of $u^2$ as the variable, we have $9x^2 = (3x)^2.$\n\nSo, let's substitute $u=3x.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm{d}x}=3\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\dfrac 1 3 \\,\\textrm d u=\\textrm d x.\n$$\n\nWe can now write the integral in terms of $u,$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{9}{4\\sqrt{1-9{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{9}{4}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-(3x)^{2}}}\\,\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{4}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{12}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}\\cdot \\operatorname{arcsin}⁡u+C \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡3x+C\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "de1e3f3a-5a64-573f-8fb1-9952fa36a839" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050628} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050675}] :knowledge-point/level-coverage [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487}] :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186682782} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/re