/id #edb/ref 17592186680116} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186680118} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186680130} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186680136} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186680137} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186680141} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186680151} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186680162} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186680165} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186680174} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186680175} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186680187} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186680191} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186680202} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186680207} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186680219} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186680225} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186682899} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186682902} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186682903} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186682908} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186682909} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186682910} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186682926} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186682939} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186682962} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186682963} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186682970} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186682979} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186682983} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186682989} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186682992} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186682996}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Indefinite Integrals Using Substitution with Inverse Secant"} :step/id #uuid "1c6cd5c4-2775-585d-9234-d995757ade98" :step/math-academy-id 641171 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065079}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065182 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065381 :tutorial/content "In this lesson, we learned how to use substitution to evaluate integrals that give rise to inverse trigonometric functions.\n\nAlthough you can always solve these integrals by going through the substitution process, it is convenient to remember the following key results:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-(ax)^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{a}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(ax)+C \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{a}^{2}-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{a}\\right)+C \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{1+(ax)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{a}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(ax)+C \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{{a}^{2}+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{a}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{a}\\right)+C \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{|ax|\\sqrt{(ax)^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{a}\\text{arcsec}(ax)+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo remember the results above, note the following:\n\n- Whenever the integrand has an $ax$ term, the argument of the resulting inverse trigonometric function is $ax.$\n\n- Otherwise, if the $a$ and $x$ are separated, then the argument of the resulting inverse trigonometric function is $\\dfrac{x}{a}.$\n\n- All the results are multiplied by $\\dfrac{1}{a},$ except for $\\arcsin \\left( \\dfrac{x}{a} \\right) + C.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "02539765-4345-5409-87f9-9901a59b7ebf" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4800 :tutorial/title "Summary of Key Results"} :step/id #uuid "b420ebdd-a5d6-5ce1-8cde-23990b2968f7" :step/math-academy-id 641177}] :activity/title "Integration by Substitution With Inverse Trigonometric Functions" :activity/type {:db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186064411}]}
